<launch>
  <!-- LEO Rover — MINS bring-up (multisensor-aided inertial navigation).
       Requires BOTH workspaces sourced before launch:
         source /home/lab272/TOUT/catkin_ws/devel/setup.bash   (leo_navigation)
         source /home/lab272/TOUT/mins_ws/devel/setup.bash     (mins)
       MINS publishes /mins/imu/odom, which pose_selector republishes onto
       /robot_pose_fused when you switch the cockpit's Pose Source to MINS.

       imu_sanitizer, carolus_vicon_bridge and wheel_remap used to live in
       this file but moved to navigation_supervision.launch so they get
       respawn="true" alongside pose_selector and leo_backend — this file
       now only owns the MINS estimator itself.

       MINS IS respawn="true", NOT required="true" — CORRIGÉ 2026-07-29.
       Cet en-tête affirmait le contraire ("stays required=true, a crash here
       should tear down the stack") et contredisait donc la déclaration du
       nœud vingt lignes plus bas, qui porte respawn="true" depuis le
       2026-07-06. Un commentaire qui contredit le code est pire qu'absent :
       il fait confiance au lecteur pour ne pas vérifier.

       POURQUOI le choix a changé : required="true" a été observé faisant
       tomber TOUTE la pile — leo_backend compris, donc le pilotage — sur un
       simple abort de MINS (assertion SystemManager.cpp:395 sur un horodatage
       IMU rétrograde, 2026-07-06). L'opérateur perdait la capacité de
       CONDUIRE le robot parce qu'un estimateur avait hoqueté. Principe retenu
       depuis : le pilotage ne doit JAMAIS dépendre de la santé d'un
       estimateur.

       CONTREPARTIE ASSUMÉE : au respawn MINS réinitialise à une NOUVELLE
       origine, donc /mins/imu/odom saute. La correction SE(3) de
       pose_selector n'absorbe PAS ce saut (elle ne compense qu'au changement
       de source), d'où une discontinuité visible sur /robot_pose_fused. Une
       trajectoire discontinue se récupère ; un cockpit mort en pleine
       manœuvre non. Voir l'annexe D (Launch file architecture) du rapport. -->

  <arg name="config" default="leo"/>   <!-- mins/config/<config>/config.yaml -->

  <!-- MINS estimator (from mins_ws).
       NOTE: passed via args="...", NOT <param name="config_path">. On ROS1,
       run_subscribe.cpp only reads argv[1] for the config path (the
       node->get_parameter() call that would read the "config_path" ROS param
       is inside an `#if ROS_AVAILABLE == 2` block — upstream ROS1 build never
       reads it). Using <param> silently fails: argv[1] becomes roslaunch's own
       auto-appended "__name:=mins_subscribe" instead, and MINS reports
       "unable to open the configuration file!" for that literal string.
       args="..." places our path at argv[1] before roslaunch's remap args. -->
  <!-- launch-prefix="stdbuf -oL": MINS's PRINT2/PRINT1 diagnostic lines (init
       status, feature-deletion counts, etc.) go through plain stdout. When
       that's redirected to a file/pipe (as roslaunch and nohup both do),
       glibc switches from line- to fully-buffered stdio, so with this low an
       output volume the [INIT] messages can sit unflushed for many minutes —
       looking exactly like "nothing is happening" when it may not be. Forces
       line buffering so diagnostics appear as they're printed. -->
  <!-- nice -n -10: limits.conf grants lab272 nice=-10 (verified 2026-07-04,
       ulimit -e = 30) — protects the estimator from CPU starvation by IDE
       indexers (cpptools), the root cause of past "zombie" episodes. -->
  <!-- respawn, NOT required: required="true" meant any MINS abort (e.g. the
       SystemManager.cpp:395 clone_time assert on a backward IMU stamp,
       observed live 2026-07-06) tore down the WHOLE stack — including
       leo_backend, i.e. cockpit driving died with the estimator. Driving must
       never depend on estimator health. On respawn MINS re-initializes at its
       own origin: /mins/imu/odom jumps, which the pose_selector SE(3)
       correction does NOT absorb (it only corrects on source switch) — an
       accepted tradeoff until MINS-side persistence exists. -->
  <node name="mins_subscribe" pkg="mins" type="subscribe" output="screen"
        clear_params="true" respawn="true" respawn_delay="5"
        launch-prefix="nice -n -10 stdbuf -oL"
        args="$(find mins)/config/$(arg config)/config.yaml">
    <!-- TF isolé (audit 2026-07-08) : MINS diffuse global->imu à ~85 Hz, ce
         qui VOLAIT le frame `imu` de l'URDF (un enfant TF n'a qu'un parent)
         et entrera en guerre avec le global->imu d'OpenVINS. Ses TF ne
         servent qu'à la visualisation -> canal dédié /tf_mins (RViz peut s'y
         abonner explicitement). -->
    <remap from="/tf" to="/tf_mins"/>
  </node>
</launch>
